{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788393749,"nonce":"hAgmB+1IB0EbG3Smu424mVBIwoA=","title":"Dev suite needs organization and a runtime toggle: today it is 17 loose verbs with no arming gate","message":"## What\n\n`rwa verbs` publishes the god-hand as ~17 flat `dev:*` entries plus two\nfixtures (`pawn-fixture`, `world-fixture`) sitting in the same namespace as\nthe ~44 player verbs, with no grouping, no listing, and no way to arm or\ndisarm them for a session.\n\n    dev:add-hediff  dev:damage  dev:destroy  dev:faction-goodwill\n    dev:finish-research  dev:guest-status  dev:heal  dev:incident\n    dev:mental-state  dev:remove-hediff  dev:set-need  dev:set-skill\n    dev:spawn-pawn  dev:spawn-thing  dev:starter-kit  dev:unfog  dev:weather\n    pawn-fixture  world-fixture\n\nThe only gate is `Prefs.DevMode`, which is a bench-wide setting that is ON for\nthe whole agent bench. So during a *play* run — where the contract is that the\ncolony is played, not staged — every cheat is one call away and nothing\ndistinguishes \"I am staging a test\" from \"I am playing\".\n\n## Why it matters\n\nDESIGN's rule is `dev:*` may bypass a widget gate, a player verb may not. That\nrule is about the ACTION MODEL. It says nothing about WHEN the god-hand should\nbe reachable, and there is no mechanism that answers it.\n\nObserved on run `openrun-20260902`, day 9: an agent called `pawn-fixture` with\nno args while probing its signature and wounded a colonist, stacked -40 of\n`Debug bad thought`, and destroyed three garments' durability on a map with\nzero cloth. The repair then used `dev:heal`, `dev:spawn-thing` x3 and\n`dev:set-need` — four more cheats — to undo one. Six god-hand calls landed in a\nplay run, none of them intended as play. Every one is in the journal, which is\nhow it was caught, but nothing *prevented* it and nothing marked the run as\ncontaminated.\n\n## Proposal\n\n1. **A `dev` listing verb** — `dev` with no args returns the catalogue: every\n   `dev:*` and fixture verb, its one-line effect, whether it mutates, and its\n   current armed state. Today the only way to learn the suite is to read\n   `Source/AutoRimmer/Dev*.cs`.\n2. **A session arming toggle** — `dev-arm {on|off, reason}`, journalled with a\n   required reason exactly like `alert-mute` and `threat-pardon`. Default\n   **off** on a session whose run has a contract. While disarmed every `dev:*`\n   and fixture call refuses with `error.class: refused` and a hint naming the\n   toggle. `Prefs.DevMode` stays the outer gate; this is the inner one.\n3. **Group them in `verbs`** — `verbs` returns a flat array today; split the\n   payload into `player`, `dev` and `fixture` so an agent reading the surface\n   cannot mistake a fixture for an observation verb.\n4. **Mark the run** — once any `dev:*` or fixture call has run, the digest\n   carries `dev_used: {count, first_tick, verbs[]}`, so a report cannot claim a\n   clean run without the contradiction being visible in the same envelope the\n   loop already reads. This is the half that matters most for the record.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `dev` with no args returns every `dev:*` and fixture verb with `mutates`\n  and `armed` per row; no such call mutates anything.\n- With the session disarmed (the default under a run contract),\n  `dev:damage`, `dev:heal`, `dev:spawn-thing`, `pawn-fixture` and\n  `world-fixture` each return `ok:false`, `error.class:\"refused\"`, and a\n  `hint` naming `dev-arm`. Game state is byte-identical across the refusal.\n- `dev-arm {on:true, reason:\"…\"}` refuses without a `reason`, writes one\n  journal `action` row carrying the reason, and flips the refusals above to\n  successes. `dev-arm {on:false}` restores them.\n- `verbs` returns `player`, `dev` and `fixture` as separate lists whose union\n  is the old flat array and whose intersection is empty.\n- After any armed dev call, `digest.dev_used.count` is \u003e= 1 and\n  `digest.dev_used.verbs` names it; on a session that never armed, the key is\n  absent (presence is the signal, per the `layouts_unenclosed` convention).\n- Arming state is per session and does NOT survive a reload — a load comes back\n  disarmed, and a test asserts that.","files":null}]}